2 CONT '''Gift 2:o''' 1662-07-09 i Stockholm med riksrådet och presidenten friherre [https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gustaf_Posse '''Gustaf Posse af Hedensund'''].
2 CONT
2 CONT /////
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M%C3%A4rta_Berendes '''Märta Berendes'''] was a Swedish Baroness and diary writer. She served as [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_Court_Mistress överhovmästarinna] ([https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki
2 CONC /Mistress_of_the_Robes Mistress of the Robes]) at the Swedish royal court from 1693 to 1717. She is the author of a diary written between 1676 and 1698, which has been published and has been published subject to research.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Life===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Märta Berendes was''' the daughter of the nobleman Johan Berendes (d. 1652) and Ingeborg Kurck (d. 1654). She inherited a fortune after her father's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1656, she married''' her uncle's brother-in-law, baron '''Johan Sparre of Bohnerö and Walstanäs''' (d. 1659). She describes this marriage as happy, but her spouse was sickly, and left her a widow with two children.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1662, she married''' for the second time to the riksråd baron '''Gustaf Posse af Hedensund till Arnäs''' (1626-1676). She described this marriage also as happy, but her second spouse, like the first, was sickly, and he and f
2 CONC our of her children all died of smallpox in 1675-76. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''After her second widowhood''', she attended to the management of her fortune and estates to support her children, until she was given an office at court in 1687.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''She had eleven children''', but only two survived her. In 1689 she lost her two adult sons, '''Axel Sparre''' and '''Carl Gustaf Posse''', who fell in battle in military service on the continent, and in 1698 she lost her daught
2 CONC er '''Margareta''' in childbirth.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Court career===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Märta Berendes served''' as [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maid_of_honour maid of honour] to queen [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christina,_Queen_of_Sweden Christina of Sweden] in 1652-1654, and to the next queen, [https://en.
2 CONC wikipedia.org/wiki/Hedwig_Eleonora_of_Holstein-Gottorp Hedvig Eleonora of Holstein-Gottorp], in 1654-1656, her aunt Kerstin Kurck having already held that position. In her autobiography, she commented that she did not wish to beco
2 CONC me a maid of honour as she disliked the life at court and accepted the office only to avoid offending the queen, and that she in fact seldom served, having to take leave to attend her sick mother.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From 1687 until 1715''', she served as Mistress of the Robes to the queen dowager Hedvig Eleonora. After the death of the queen, [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ulrika_Eleonora_of_Denmark Ulrika Eleonora of Denmark] in 1693, the
2 CONC  queen dowager was in effect the first lady of the Swedish court, making Berendes in effect first in rank of all female courtiers.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From 1693 until her''' death, she had was additionally given the office of överhovmästarinna (in this case in effect that of royal governess) at the court of the princesses, [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hedvig_Sophia_of_Swede
2 CONC n Hedvig Sophia of Sweden] and [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ulrika_Eleonora,_Queen_of_Sweden Ulrika Eleonora of Sweden].
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Berendes was described''' as a melancholic and passionately religious widow.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''She had an important''' position at court, being treated with familiarity by the royal family: King [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_XII_of_Sweden Charles XII] of Sweden referred to her as 'Old Aunt'.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1709, she participated''' in the court [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camarilla camarilla] consisting of herself, [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arvid_Horn Arvid Horn], the queen dowager's priest Molin, [https://en.wikipedia
2 CONC .org/wiki/Beata_Sparre Beata Sparre] and [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christina_Piper Christina Piper] who formed an alliance with Carl Gyllenstierna and successfully evicted [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anna_Catharina_von_B%C
2 CONC 3%A4rfelt Anna Catharina von Bärfelt] from court.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''She died in Stockholm''' in 1717, her funeral service took place at the Riddarholm Church in Stockholm, but her final resting place is at St. Nicholas Church in Arboga, beside her husband Posse and his other wife.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Author===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Märta Berendes wrote''' her autobiography or diary in the back pages of her prayer book, which recounts her life from the death of her first spouse in 1659 until the death of her daughter in 1698, when the pages of her prayer b
2 CONC ook ended.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''It is written in the form''' of a litany of sorrows, depicting her misfortune caused by the death of her husbands and children, describing life as a martyrdom possible to endure only by the help of religion. Her description of 
2 CONC her life centers on the sorrow she felt for the illness of her mother, the illnesses and deaths of her first and second husband, and the deaths of all but two of her eleven children.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''It has been published''' and is subject to research.
2 CONT
2 CONT /////
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://nordicwomensliterature.net/2011/08/25/now-the-lord-shall-take-me-too/ Now the Lord Shall Take Me Too], further reading on Märta Berendes.
2 CONT <br/>
2 CONT <br/>
