1 NOTE During his minority the government was entrusted to a regency, and 
2 CONT although the kingdom was kept free from foreign wars, it was misgoverned. 
2 CONT The education of the young king was so neglected that he was nearly 
2 CONT illiterate. In 1672 he assumed the reins of government. Under terms of an 
2 CONT agreement made by the regency, Charles in 1674 entered the Dutch Wars as 
2 CONT an ally of Louis XIV, king of France. The Swedish army and navy were 
2 CONT unprepared for war, and Sweden lost territory, although much of it was 
2 CONT restored by the Peace of Saint-Germain-en-Laye in 1679. Charles, angered 
2 CONT by the military failure, and supported by the burghers and peasants, 
2 CONT instituted reforms that strengthened the armed forces and considerably 
2 CONT reduced the power of the former regents and nobles. In 1682 the Riksdag, 
2 CONT the Swedish legislature, granted the king absolute authority. By a 
2 CONT judicious administration of revenues, he wiped out the public debt, 
2 CONT reorganized the army and navy, and by 1693 was able to dispense with 
2 CONT extraordinary subsidies. Although he had absolute power, he never imposed 
2 CONT a tax without consent; and he published an annual account of revenues and 
2 CONT expenditures. He was succeeded by his son Charles XII.
