1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Maitland,_1st_Earl_of_Lauderdale
2 CONT
2 CONT John Maitland, 1st Earl of Lauderdale, Viscount of Lauderdale, Viscount Maitland, and Lord Thirlestane and Boltoun, (died January 1645) was President of the Scottish Parliament as well as the Privy Council, a lawyer and a judge, w
2 CONC ho sided with the Parliamentarian cause during the Civil War.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of Sir John Maitland, 1st Lord Thirlestane by his spouse Jean, only daughter and heiress of the Fourth Lord Fleming (subsequently Countess of Cassillis), he was admitted a member of the Privy Council of Scotland on 20 July
2 CONC  1615.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 2 April 1616 he was created Viscount of Lauderdale, by Letters Patent, to him and his heirs male and successors in the lordship of Thirlestane.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was subsequently made President of the Privy Council, and was appointed an Ordinary Lord of Session on 5 June 1618. He was at that time one of the Commissioners for the Plantation of Kirks.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 14 March 1624, at Whitehall, London, he was created, by patent, Earl of Lauderdale, Viscount Maitland, and Lord Thirlestane and Boltoun.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Lauderdale was removed from his place on the bench, on 14 February 1626, in consequence of a resolution by King Charles I that no nobleman should hold the seat of an ordinary Lord, and instead was on 1 June following appointe
2 CONC d one of the Extraordinary Lords of Session, usually reserved by the Crown for either noblemen or dignitaries of The Church. He remained an Extraordinary Lord until 8 November 1628, and in the following year was appointed one of t
2 CONC he Lords of the Articles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Regardless of the honours generously bestowed upon him by his monarch, upon the breaking out of the English Civil War, he joined the side of the parliament and was employed in a great variety of commissions of importance.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 4 June 1644 he was elected President of the parliament, and reappointed on 7 January following. He died before the 20th of the same month, and was interred in the Maitland family burial vault within St. Mary's Collegiate Church
2 CONC , Haddington.
2 CONT
2 CONT A poetical epitaph on him by Drummond of Hawthornden, as also the one by King James VI on his father, the Chancellor, can be found in Crawfurd's Peerage.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Lady Isabel Seton (d. November 1638), daughter of Alexander Seton, 1st Earl of Dunfermline, celebrated by Arthur Johnston in his poems. They had a large family of whom only three sons and one daughter survived their par
2 CONC ents.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT John Maitland, 1st Earl of Lauderdale was the son of John Maitland, 1st Baron Maitland of Thirlestane and Jean Fleming. He married Lady Isabel Seton, daughter of Alexander Seton, 1st Earl of Dunfermline and Grizel Leslie, before 1
2 CONC 8 June 1610.1 He died on 18 January 1644/45.1
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT created Viscount of Lauderdale in 1616, and Earl of Lauderdale etc., in 1624
